{"module":"sovereignty","voice":"male","duration":188.4,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Native nations today: sovereignty, land and justice","start":0.0,"end":4.09,"words":[[0.05,0.51],[0.51,1.05],[1.05,1.51],[1.78,2.55],[2.82,3.12],[3.12,3.35],[3.35,3.89]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Native nations are not only history.","start":4.89,"end":7.31,"words":[[4.93,5.3],[5.3,5.74],[5.74,5.8],[5.8,5.99],[5.99,6.31],[6.31,6.81]]},{"el":"intro","text":"They are governments today, with their own laws, courts and police, and their rights rest on treaties the Constitution calls “the supreme law of the land.”","start":7.66,"end":18.24,"words":[[7.71,7.96],[7.96,8.05],[8.05,8.89],[8.89,9.39],[9.68,9.93],[9.93,10.19],[10.19,10.44],[10.44,10.77],[11.07,11.4],[11.4,11.65],[11.65,12.16],[12.45,12.7],[12.7,12.95],[12.95,13.37],[13.37,13.71],[13.71,13.88],[13.88,14.38],[14.38,14.55],[14.55,15.55],[15.55,15.89],[15.89,16.06],[16.06,16.56],[16.56,16.73],[16.73,16.89],[16.89,17.06],[17.06,17.48]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Nations, not minorities","start":18.59,"end":21.96,"words":[[18.64,19.66],[20.1,20.48],[20.48,21.75]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The U.S. recognizes more than 570 tribal nations, each with its own government.","start":22.66,"end":29.41,"words":[[22.71,22.85],[22.85,23.2],[23.54,24.31],[24.31,24.45],[24.45,24.66],[24.66,26.06],[26.06,26.55],[26.55,27.1],[27.35,27.56],[27.56,27.77],[27.77,27.98],[27.98,28.19],[28.19,28.88]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Their relationship with the federal government is government-to-government, grounded in treaties and in a federal “trust responsibility.”","start":29.76,"end":38.04,"words":[[29.8,30.01],[30.01,30.77],[30.77,30.98],[30.98,31.12],[31.12,31.6],[31.6,32.22],[32.22,32.36],[32.36,33.81],[34.05,34.6],[34.6,34.74],[34.74,35.15],[35.15,35.36],[35.36,35.5],[35.5,35.64],[35.64,36.12],[36.12,36.46],[36.46,37.5]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Article VI of the Constitution makes treaties part of the supreme law of the land.","start":38.39,"end":43.56,"words":[[38.44,38.87],[38.87,39.23],[39.23,39.38],[39.38,39.52],[39.52,40.39],[40.39,40.75],[40.75,41.18],[41.18,41.4],[41.4,41.54],[41.54,41.68],[41.68,42.12],[42.12,42.26],[42.26,42.41],[42.41,42.55],[42.55,42.91]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Who can prosecute crimes","start":44.46,"end":46.35,"words":[[44.5,44.66],[44.66,44.9],[44.9,45.64],[45.64,46.12]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Oliphant (1978) the Supreme Court held that tribes can't prosecute non-Native people, even for crimes on tribal land, leaving many crimes against Native women unprosecuted.","start":47.05,"end":60.31,"words":[[47.1,47.26],[47.26,47.8],[47.8,49.21],[49.48,49.64],[49.64,50.11],[50.11,50.34],[50.34,50.65],[50.65,50.89],[50.89,51.36],[51.36,51.67],[51.67,52.37],[52.37,53.07],[53.07,53.54],[53.82,54.13],[54.13,54.28],[54.28,54.75],[54.75,54.91],[54.91,55.46],[55.46,55.85],[56.12,56.51],[56.51,56.82],[56.82,57.29],[57.29,57.76],[57.76,58.23],[58.23,58.62],[58.62,59.71]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Congress restored limited tribal jurisdiction over domestic violence in 2013 and expanded it in 2022.","start":60.66,"end":68.7,"words":[[60.7,61.2],[61.2,61.63],[61.63,62.13],[62.13,62.64],[62.64,63.57],[63.57,63.86],[63.86,64.43],[64.43,65.0],[65.0,65.15],[65.15,65.94],[65.94,66.15],[66.15,66.8],[66.8,66.94],[66.94,67.09],[67.09,68.16]]},{"el":"s1","text":"After McGirt (2020) recognized eastern Oklahoma's reservations, the Court held in Castro-Huerta (2022) that states can also prosecute non-Natives for crimes against Native people there.","start":69.05,"end":82.44,"words":[[69.1,69.4],[69.4,69.77],[69.77,70.66],[70.92,71.66],[71.66,72.03],[72.03,72.84],[72.84,73.73],[73.99,74.13],[74.13,74.36],[74.36,74.65],[74.65,74.8],[74.8,75.76],[75.76,76.8],[77.06,77.28],[77.28,77.72],[77.72,77.94],[77.94,78.31],[78.31,78.98],[78.98,79.72],[79.72,79.87],[79.87,80.31],[80.31,80.76],[80.76,81.2],[81.2,81.57],[81.57,81.87]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Children and families","start":83.34,"end":84.93,"words":[[83.39,83.99],[83.99,84.21],[84.21,84.73]]},{"el":"s2","text":"By the 1970s, about a quarter to a third of Native children had been taken from their families, mostly into non-Native homes.","start":85.63,"end":95.0,"words":[[85.68,85.91],[85.91,86.06],[86.06,87.28],[87.55,87.93],[87.93,88.08],[88.08,88.46],[88.46,88.61],[88.61,88.76],[88.76,88.99],[88.99,89.14],[89.14,89.6],[89.6,90.21],[90.21,90.44],[90.44,90.67],[90.67,91.12],[91.12,91.43],[91.43,91.66],[91.66,92.26],[92.53,93.06],[93.06,93.37],[93.37,94.05],[94.05,94.51]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Indian Child Welfare Act (1978) gave tribes a role in these cases.","start":95.35,"end":100.98,"words":[[95.39,95.53],[95.53,95.95],[95.95,96.37],[96.37,96.78],[96.78,96.99],[96.99,98.25],[98.49,98.77],[98.77,99.19],[99.19,99.33],[99.33,99.6],[99.6,99.74],[99.74,99.95],[99.95,100.44]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2023, in Haaland v. Brackeen, the Supreme Court rejected a challenge to the law.","start":101.33,"end":107.51,"words":[[101.37,101.5],[101.5,102.57],[102.8,102.94],[102.94,103.34],[103.34,103.54],[103.87,104.34],[104.57,104.7],[104.7,105.1],[105.1,105.3],[105.3,105.9],[105.9,106.04],[106.04,106.57],[106.57,106.7],[106.7,106.84],[106.84,107.03]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Water and land","start":108.41,"end":110.35,"words":[[108.46,109.09],[109.09,109.56],[109.56,110.18]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 2023 the Court ruled in Arizona v. Navajo Nation that the Navajo's 1868 treaty doesn't require the U.S. to take steps to secure water for the nation, where many homes still lack running water.","start":111.05,"end":125.82,"words":[[111.1,111.25],[111.25,112.42],[112.42,112.58],[112.58,112.81],[112.81,113.13],[113.13,113.28],[113.28,113.91],[113.91,114.14],[114.53,115.08],[115.08,115.55],[115.55,115.78],[115.78,115.94],[115.94,116.56],[116.56,117.73],[117.73,118.12],[118.12,118.59],[118.59,119.14],[119.14,119.29],[119.29,119.68],[120.07,120.23],[120.23,120.54],[120.54,120.93],[120.93,121.09],[121.09,121.56],[121.56,121.87],[121.87,122.02],[122.02,122.18],[122.18,122.73],[123.0,123.23],[123.23,123.55],[123.55,123.94],[123.94,124.25],[124.25,124.48],[124.48,124.87],[124.87,125.26]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Some land is being returned: Congress restored the National Bison Range to the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes in 2020, and the transfer was completed in 2022.","start":126.17,"end":136.83,"words":[[126.21,126.41],[126.41,126.67],[126.67,126.8],[126.8,127.12],[127.12,127.57],[127.8,128.25],[128.25,128.64],[128.64,128.77],[128.77,129.23],[129.23,129.61],[129.61,130.0],[130.0,130.13],[130.13,130.26],[130.26,131.1],[131.1,131.43],[131.43,131.62],[131.62,132.08],[132.08,132.46],[132.46,132.59],[132.59,133.44],[133.66,133.86],[133.86,133.99],[133.99,134.44],[134.44,134.63],[134.63,135.22],[135.22,135.35],[135.35,136.32]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The “Land Back” movement seeks more.","start":137.18,"end":139.51,"words":[[137.22,137.38],[137.38,137.68],[137.68,137.91],[137.91,138.45],[138.45,138.76],[138.76,138.99]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Voting","start":140.41,"end":141.1,"words":[[140.46,140.97]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Many reservation homes don't have standard street addresses.","start":141.8,"end":145.91,"words":[[141.85,142.15],[142.15,142.88],[142.88,143.25],[143.25,143.62],[143.62,143.84],[143.84,144.36],[144.36,144.73],[144.73,145.32]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 2018 North Dakota began requiring voter IDs with a residential street address, which tribal members challenged; the state settled with tribes in 2020.","start":146.26,"end":158.28,"words":[[146.3,146.46],[146.46,147.4],[147.4,147.64],[147.64,148.18],[148.18,148.58],[148.58,149.36],[149.36,149.75],[149.75,150.06],[150.06,150.3],[150.3,150.46],[150.46,151.24],[151.24,151.63],[151.63,152.1],[152.38,152.69],[152.69,153.24],[153.24,153.71],[153.71,154.49],[154.77,154.92],[154.92,155.31],[155.31,155.78],[155.78,156.02],[156.02,156.49],[156.49,156.65],[156.65,157.66]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Long distances to polling places and post offices remain barriers to voting.","start":158.63,"end":163.96,"words":[[158.68,158.91],[158.91,159.59],[159.59,159.74],[159.74,160.19],[160.19,160.72],[160.72,160.94],[160.94,161.32],[161.32,161.77],[161.77,162.23],[162.23,162.68],[162.68,162.83],[162.83,163.36]]},{"el":"h5","text":"How to be a good ally","start":164.86,"end":166.6,"words":[[164.9,165.19],[165.19,165.38],[165.38,165.58],[165.58,165.77],[165.77,166.06],[166.06,166.44]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Learn whose land you're on, and the nation's own history and current government.","start":167.3,"end":172.8,"words":[[167.34,167.59],[167.59,167.83],[167.83,168.16],[168.16,168.32],[168.32,168.57],[168.86,169.1],[169.1,169.27],[169.27,169.84],[169.84,170.09],[170.09,170.66],[170.66,170.91],[170.91,171.4],[171.4,172.22]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Support Native-led organizations rather than speaking for them.","start":173.15,"end":177.01,"words":[[173.2,173.56],[173.56,174.19],[174.19,175.1],[175.1,175.39],[175.39,175.6],[175.6,176.02],[176.02,176.16],[176.16,176.44]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Back tribal sovereignty in policy debates, including over policing and courts on tribal land.","start":177.36,"end":184.67,"words":[[177.42,177.67],[177.67,178.27],[178.27,179.04],[179.04,179.21],[179.21,179.72],[179.72,180.41],[180.71,181.39],[181.39,181.73],[181.73,182.33],[182.33,182.59],[182.59,182.93],[182.93,183.1],[183.1,183.7],[183.7,184.12]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Take the quest “Learn whose land you're on.”","start":185.02,"end":187.54,"words":[[185.07,185.37],[185.37,185.52],[185.52,185.89],[185.89,186.12],[186.12,186.34],[186.34,186.64],[186.64,186.79],[186.79,187.01]]}]}